    "case_id": "702",
    "case_summary": "A 45-year-old woman presented with severe crampy pains in hypogastrium radiating down the back of the left thigh, accompanied by nausea and vomiting. She found a fist-sized bulging in her left buttock area that was painful to touch, forcing her to maintain a bowed position while walking. For the past year, she experienced occasional attacks of abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting lasting 30 minutes to an hour, relieved by purgative enema and analgesic injection. Medical history included calculous cholecystitis and cesarean delivery sixteen years ago.\n\nVital signs were stable. Patient appeared in acute pain, dehydrated, and poorly nourished. She maintained knee-flexed position due to pain. Abdomen was soft with epigastrium tenderness, rebounding pain, and absence of intestinal gurgling sounds. A non-reducible, tender mass measuring 10 cm × 8 cm × 8 cm was present in the left buttock area.\n\n- Laboratory Tests: Not specifically mentioned in the case report.- Imaging Studies:  1. Abdominal X-ray:     - Showed intestinal obstruction and gallstone    2. Transgluteal ultrasonography:     - Revealed incarcerated bowel loops beneath left gluteal muscles    3. CT examination:     - Demonstrated seroperitoneum, pneumoperitoneum, low intestinal obstruction     - Image Title: CT manifestation     - Image Description: Shows herniation through left sciatic foramen containing small bowel loop",
